00:00:00.230 --> 00:00:02.550 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  monday september 19th i'm zoe thomas for  the wall street journal  joseph sullivan was the chief security  officer of uber back in 2016 when the  ride-hailing company experienced a  massive data breach  the names emails and phone numbers of  millions of riders were exposed along  with around 600 000 driver's license  numbers  a year later uber disclosed the breach  and said it paid the hackers one hundred  thousand dollars  to get paid the hackers were made to  sign a non-disclosure agreement saying  they'd destroyed the data  sullivan was fired from the company in  2017 and he's now on trial for how he  handled uber's response to the hacking  he's accused of criminal obstruction and  he's pleaded not guilty  what happens to sullivan in this trial  is being closely watched by his peers in  the cybersecurity space  joining us to discuss the trial and its  implications is wsj cyber security  reporter robert mcmillan  hi bob thanks for joining us yeah great  to be here  bob companies are hit with hacks and  ransomware attacks pretty often these  days so what makes prosecutors allege  that in this case a crime was committed  well in this case there was an ftc  investigation going on so companies  aren't necessarily always under the  scrutiny of the federal government when  they get hacked and the feds want  everybody to know to be on notice that  they're taking this stuff seriously so  the allegations here are that mr  sullivan  basically engaged in a series of  behaviors that ultimately misled the ftc  about what was going on with  cyber security at uber and specifically  they're saying he paid some hackers a  hundred thousand dollars to make a data  breach go away  which is definitely a no-no now mr  sullivan's side of the story is  look it we get security problems  reported all the time there's this thing  called a bug bounty where we just pay  outside researchers to report issues to  us and we felt that  the data was not you know leaked we had  control of the data and we just sort of  funneled this notification of a problem  into our bug bounty program and resolve  the issue with no risk to consumers  i think the trial is interesting even if  you're not super into cyber crime or  cyber security but you know for those of  us who are maybe less aware of how these  systems kind of work can you explain  what a bug bounty is because i think  that's kind of the crux of what's going  on here did he pay a bug bounty or did  he pay ransom  so in olden times hackers would find  problems with people's software or  websites and they would just out of the  goodness of their heart many times try  to get them fixed or else they would  maybe exploit them for  for fun or profit you know there are  sort of two ways people could go with  this kind of security information and a  few years ago the tech company started  realizing like hey we could incentivize  these hackers to just quietly report  these issues to us and fix them but we  need to give them some incentives so in  the olden days it was like you get a  t-shirt for reporting a bug but hacking  has become big business and so  companies are paying out hundreds of  thousands of dollars now  for security information if you find a  bug that's truly devastating to the you  know apple iphone you can make a lot of  money with that information so this sort  of  gray kind of business has evolved where  people have you know they're sort of  poking around on your website they find  a problem and well they could hack in or  they could sell it to you know a nation  state that could then hack into this  company or they could report it to the  company and make some money  how do the decisions to pay out a sum  like this typically get made in a  company  well there's no question that in this  case it was an unusual decision so the  biggest bug bounty whoever had paid up  previous to this incident was ten  thousand dollars and suddenly they're  paying out a hundred thousand dollars  ten times what they've paid before so  this decision we know from corp records  that it went up to the ceo level  sullivan and the ceo discussed it the  payment decision can be just sort of an  automatic thing it doesn't have to be  discussed at that level you know you set  up the program and you have the  parameters of what you're going to be  paying and it doesn't have to be a ceo  level call  to pay you know ten thousand dollars for  a bug but this case was a little unusual  and so that's actually one of the things  that the prosecutors are pointing out  and drawing attention to  so who have we heard from so far in this  trial and how has that shaped either  side's argument through the court case  we're going to hear from  uber's security team the incident  responders the people that were dealing  with this problem and according to mr  sullivan's team there are more than 30  people who were in the loop about what  was going on there we're also going to  hear from the legal team the ones who  are interfacing with the ftc  and making the decisions about what to  tell them  and on friday uber's ceo testified about  the breach  basically he said he was hired in august  of 2017 and when he took over he  kind of took a look at what was said  about this incident and he  lost confidence in joe sullivan he felt  like he couldn't trust what sullivan was  telling him so that's why he fired him  okay the cyber security community is  watching this case pretty closely what  are the implications they're worried  about so the sullivan case is really  being watched very carefully by a lot of  security executives because he's facing  jail time for making a decision that i  think they have a lot of sympathy with  this like you have got a lot of pressure  when you're in that position  and the calls that get made you know  they involve a lot of people we're  talking about the ceo  the legal team and the security team all  in concert kind of making the decisions  on what to say to the ftc and how to  respond to this breach  and you're the security guy's the one  facing jail time so  there definitely is a contingent of  people who work in this business who  really think  that mr sullivan  should not have been charged right  because he had a hard job he had to make  a call  and um  you know they really have sympathy for  the pressure he was under now there's  another group of people who say like  look at it's obviously a data breach  when somebody gets the data and  downloads it from your company you got  to disclose that and why didn't that  happen  all right that was wsj cyber security  reporter robert mcmillan bob thanks  always for joining us great to talk to  you all  and that's it for today's tech news  briefing if you want more tech stories  check out our website wsj.com  i'm zoe thomas for the wall street  journal  thanks for listening  [Music]  [Music]  you

